---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import TerminalSnippet from '../../components/TerminalSnippet.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '../../utils/paths.js';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Install JobFoundry the easy way (AppImage / MSIX, no terminal needed) or with Docker — then add the browser extension and start capturing jobs."
  currentPath={resolvePath('/docs/getting-started/')}
  category="Installation & Setup"
>
  <h1>Getting Started with JobFoundry</h1>
  <p>
    You do <strong>not</strong> need to know programming, Docker, or AI tools to use
    JobFoundry. Pick the easiest install method for your computer below, add the
    browser extension, and keep browsing job sites like you always have —
    capture, scoring, and tailoring happen automatically in the background.
  </p>

  <div class="doc-callout success">
    <div class="callout-title">
      <span>✓</span>
      <span>What you need</span>
    </div>
    <p>
      A computer (Linux, Windows 11, or macOS), a browser (Chrome, Edge, Brave,
      or Firefox), and — only if you want fit scores and tailored resumes — an
      LLM API key (e.g. OpenRouter) <em>or</em> a free local model via Ollama.
      Job capture alone needs nothing else.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Method 1: Ready-made app (easiest, no terminal)</h2>
  <p>
    Download the latest release from the
    <a href="https://github.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Releases page</a>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Linux:</strong> download <code>JobFoundry-&lt;version&gt;-x86_64.AppImage</code>,
      right-click → Properties → allow executing (or run
      <code>chmod +x JobFoundry-*.AppImage</code> once), then double-click to
      launch. The dashboard opens at
      <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>http://localhost:8080</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windows 11:</strong> download <code>JobFoundry-&lt;version&gt;-x64.msix</code>
      and <code>JobFoundry.cer</code>. Right-click the Start button → Terminal
      (Admin) and trust the certificate once, then install the package:
    </li>
  </ul>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="Import-Certificate -FilePath .\JobFoundry.cer -CertStoreLocation Cert:\LocalMachine\TrustedPeople"
    title="TRUST CERTIFICATE ONCE (ADMIN POWERSHELL)"
  />
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="Add-AppxPackage -Path .\JobFoundry-<version>-x64.msix"
    title="INSTALL JOBFOUNDRY"
  />
  <p>
    Then launch <strong>JobFoundry</strong> from the Start menu. Your data and
    settings live under <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\JobFoundry\</code>, safely outside
    the app package.
  </p>

  <h2>Method 2: One-line install with Docker</h2>
  <p>
    On Linux, macOS, or Windows (WSL2) with Docker installed, this single command
    sets up everything — container, configuration, and health check:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry/main/install.sh | bash"
    title="ONE-LINE INSTALL"
  />
  <p>
    Then open <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>http://localhost:8080</code></a>
    in your browser to see the dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Method 3: Manual setup (developers)</h2>
  <p>
    If you prefer to manage services yourself or contribute code:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="git clone https://github.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry.git && cd JobFoundry"
    title="CLONE REPOSITORY"
  />
  <p>
    Copy the sample configuration and add your LLM provider key:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="cp .env.example .env"
    title="CONFIGURE ENVIRONMENT"
  />
  <p>
    Start everything in the background:
  </p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="docker compose up -d"
    title="START SERVICES (DOCKER)"
  />
  <p>Or with Podman:</p>
  <TerminalSnippet
    command="podman compose up -d"
    title="START SERVICES (PODMAN)"
  />
  <p>
    For bare-metal development without containers (Node.js 26+, Python 3.12+,
    <code>uv</code>), see the <a href="https://github.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry/blob/main/DEVELOPMENT.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Development Guide</a>
    for per-service commands.
  </p>

  <h2>Next: add the browser extension</h2>
  <p>
    The extension is what captures job postings as you browse — the server
    cannot do this part (by design). Download the prebuilt
    <code>.zip</code> for your browser from the
    <a href="https://github.com/deadrat-in/JobFoundry/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Releases page</a>
    (no building required) and load it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chrome / Edge / Brave:</strong> open <code>chrome://extensions/</code>, enable <strong>Developer mode</strong>, click <strong>Load unpacked</strong>, and select the unzipped folder.</li>
    <li><strong>Firefox:</strong> open <code>about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox</code>, click <strong>Load Temporary Add-on…</strong>, and select the manifest file.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Then: upload your resume and browse</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the dashboard at <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>http://localhost:8080</code></a>.</li>
    <li>Go to the <strong>Resume Manager</strong> and upload your master resume (JSON Resume format).</li>
    <li>If you want fit scores and tailored resumes, add your LLM key in <strong>Settings</strong> (or the <code>.env</code> file for manual installs).</li>
    <li>Browse job sites normally. Listings flow into the dashboard, get scored, and appear on your Kanban board.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="next-step-box">
    <span class="next-label">NEXT STEP</span>
    <a href={resolvePath('/docs/extension/')} class="next-link">
      Install the Companion Browser Extension →
    </a>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .next-step-box {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background-color: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-cyan);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .next-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .next-link {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent-cyan);
  }
</style>
